KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ two steps: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ one step: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.spec.partial.match]/1, [temp.constr.decl]/1: impl<int, 2>
derives from impl<int, 3>, which derives from impl<int, 4>, the constrained
partial specialization.  The front end fails silently ("could not fully
type-check 'main'", no other diagnostic) when the chain needs two steps and
impl<int, 3> was not instantiated before; with one step, an unconstrained
partial specialization, or impl<int, 3> instantiated first, it works.
Root cause of cpp20_common_reference_int_lvalue_rvalue failing with the
libstdc++ 16 headers: common_reference<int &, long &> walks
__common_reference_impl's bullets 1-4 this way (instantiating
__common_reference_impl<int &, long &, 4>, 3 and 2 first makes that test
pass).  test_unsatisfied.desc is the libstdc++ shape with unsatisfied
constrained partial specializations on the way; it crashes (unbounded
recursion in primary_base / vtable_pointer_component).  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 accept both programs, and they fail only their WRONG assertion.
